@@ -0,0 +1,74 @@
+# Issue #1 leftover ridge
+
+Confirmed on the nine-vertex basin notch and rechecked against both original
+EPSG:32637 basins and the issue #2 river.
+
+## What drops the notch
+
+`intersection_velocity` treated a non-finite result or a speed above `1e6` as
+a missing intersection. The notch is edges 4 and 6: opposing supports,
+`|sin| ≈ 1.010e-7`, finite speed about `1.9802574214462075e7`. The cap
+replaced that motion with a zero velocity, so the leftover ridge never
+reached its partner. Construction still returned `Ok`. Exported coordinates
+formed two components.
+
+Deleting the cap alone leaves the two-time subtraction `p1 - p0`. On this
+pair that subtraction is ill-conditioned, and the earliest of the three
+constraint times stops an endpoint short of the common point (about 0.82 m
+when the cap is only removed). Ancestry never joins the two sides, so the
+exact-bit graph stays disconnected.
+
+## What closes the notch
+
+Pairs whose two-time velocity is finite and at most `1e6` are unchanged.
+When that velocity is above the cap, only an opposing pair with
+`|sin| < 1e-4` may keep the normal-derived speed, and only when
+`p0 + time * vel` is already within 1 m of the birth (1e-6 at unit scale).
+That motion is then back-projected through the birth. A fast intersection
+that does not pass near the birth stays missing, as before: same-direction
+offsets use the common normal, and opposing ones get zero velocity.
+Applying the normal-derived speed to every near-parallel pair, including
+moderate speeds under the cap, broke lake 10623 (`face=11004` not reachable
+from its root). Well-conditioned speeds above the cap stay missing.
+
+A 3-constraint face keeps the earliest edge time unless a later edge time
+puts the three vertices much closer (`best_span - span > 1e-4` and
+`span < best_span * 0.5`). The notch then meets, the exported graph is one
+tree, every input corner is present, and endpoint bits match.
+
+These two changes belong together. The finite fast velocity is the motion
+the event queue has to follow. The later co-arrival time is what stops that
+motion on the shared point instead of on the first noisy 1D hit.
+
+## Direct-normal rewrite
+
+Replacing every intersection velocity with the supporting-line normal, and
+dropping the cap globally, is rejected. It looped a spoke fixture, changed
+weighted and degree-one results, broke the larger lake DCEL check, and still
+failed both original basins.
+
+## What still fails on the original basins
+
+Both basin polygons still return `IncompleteFaceCycle` (“owns N halfedges
+but has no root”), in debug and release: feature 1 face 1137 owns 71,
+feature 2 face 346 owns 7. The hole-bearing basin is not asserted to be a
+hole-free tree.
+
+While every near-parallel pair used the normal-derived velocity, feature 2's
+first far stop was a 2-constraint triangle collapse: a vertex of speed about
+`82822` (under the cap) was frozen about 2.1e6 m outside the basin while the
+other two vertices were still local. That broader substitution also made
+lake 10623 fail, so it is not kept. On the narrower rule above, the same
+validation class remains (a bounded face owns halfedges and has no
+reciprocal root).
+
+Refusing a triangle collapse whose vertices are kilometres apart moved the
+failure to another face, including a duplicate segment closed by an infinity
+chord. Refusing any span above 1 m split the exported basin graph into 75
+components. On-segment filters, stale constraint handlers, and an input-bbox
+rejection, together with those refusals, previously split the river into 144
+components. The good river is one tree, 51923 arcs, 51924 coordinate nodes,
+cycle rank 0. Those gates are not in the tree.
+
+The nine-vertex regression is the narrow velocity and 3-constraint change.
+It does not by itself give either original basin a valid bounded DCEL.
